{"id":"6c3d1bf956986b8360ccd803c89b9c2411e3d3ca","axis":"silent","repo":"oven-sh/bun","sha":"6c3d1bf956986b8360ccd803c89b9c2411e3d3ca","parsed":{"files":[{"path":"docs/runtime/archive.mdx","lines":["@@ -144,6 +144,16 @@ console.log(`Extracted ${count} entries`);"," "," **Security note**: Bun.Archive validates paths during extraction. It rejects absolute paths (POSIX `/`, Windows drive letters like `C:\\` or `C:/`, and UNC paths like `\\\\server\\share`) and unsafe symlink targets. It normalizes away path traversal components (`..`) to prevent directory escape attacks: `dir/sub/../file` becomes `dir/file`."," ","+On Linux and macOS, `extract()` also stays inside the target directory when that directory already contains symlinks, including symlinks from an archive you extracted there earlier:","+","+- Bun does not write through a symlink that leads out of the target directory, or through one with an absolute target. When a file entry needs such a symlink, `extract()` rejects, or skips the entry if you pass a glob. The `code` of the error is `\"EXDEV\"`, and its `path` is where Bun would have written the entry, relative to the target directory. Extraction stops at that entry, and what Bun has already written stays. A symlink that Bun cannot resolve at all, such as a loop, is a `ReadError` without a `code`. `extract()` skips a directory entry that needs one.","+- Bun follows a relative symlink that stays inside the target directory.","+- Bun creates a symlink entry only in a directory that it reaches without following a symlink, and skips the entry otherwise.","+","+On Windows, `extract()` does not check the junctions and symlinks that already exist in the target directory.","+","+On Linux 5.6 or later, Bun resolves these paths with the `openat2` system call, and resolves them itself where a seccomp policy answers `openat2` with `ENOSYS` or `EPERM`. If your seccomp policy kills the process instead, set `BUN_FEATURE_FLAG_DISABLE_RESOLVE_BENEATH=1` so that Bun never makes the call.","+"," ### Filtering Extracted Files"," "," Use glob patterns to extract only specific files. Bun matches patterns against archive entry paths normalized to use forward slashes (`/`). Positive patterns specify what to include, and negative patterns (prefixed with `!`) specify what to exclude. When you pass only negative patterns, Bun includes all entries that don't match them:","@@ -201,6 +211,8 @@ Each `File` object includes:"," "," **Note**: `files()` loads file contents into memory. For large archives, use `extract()` to write directly to disk instead."," ","+The holes of a sparse file take no space in the archive, and `files()` reads them as zeros. `files()` rejects once the holes it has read from one archive add up to more than 1,024 times the size of the archive as it was given, so the compressed size of a `.tar.gz`. It always reads up to 64 MiB of holes and never more than 1 GiB. `extract()` has no such limit, because it leaves holes unallocated on disk.","+"," ### Error Handling"," "," Archive operations can fail due to corrupted data, I/O errors, or invalid paths. Use try/catch to handle these cases:"],"additions":12,"deletions":0,"truncated":false},{"path":"docs/runtime/http/routing.mdx","lines":["@@ -207,7 +207,14 @@ Bun.serve({"," });"," ```"," ","-Bun percent-decodes the part of the request URL after the prefix once and opens it relative to `dir`. Bun rejects non-canonical paths with `404`, so the served path is always the path the router matched. A path is non-canonical if it contains `.`, `..`, empty segments, `%2F`, or a `%XX` sequence encoding a character that may appear literally in a path segment. On Linux the open uses `openat2(RESOLVE_IN_ROOT)`, so the kernel clamps symlinks that would escape `dir`.","+Bun percent-decodes the part of the request URL after the prefix once and opens it relative to `dir`. Bun rejects non-canonical paths with `404`, so the served path is always the path the router matched. A path is non-canonical if it contains `.`, `..`, empty segments, `%2F`, or a `%XX` sequence encoding a character that may appear literally in a path segment.","+","+A symlink never leads out of `dir`. Bun resolves symlinks as if `dir` were the root directory:","+","+- A `..` stops at `dir`.","+- An absolute target starts from `dir`. A symlink to `/var/data/logo.png` serves `var/data/logo.png` in `dir`, or is a `404` if there is no such file.","+","+These are the rules of `openat2(RESOLVE_IN_ROOT)` on Linux. Bun applies them itself on macOS, on FreeBSD, and on Linux when `openat2` is not available. On Windows, Bun follows symlinks wherever they lead."," "," <Info>","   Routing is case-sensitive, but filesystems on macOS and Windows are case-insensitive by default. As a result, a"],"additions":8,"deletions":1,"truncated":false},{"path":"packages/bun-types/bun.d.ts","lines":["@@ -10752,6 +10752,10 @@ declare module \"bun\" {","      * Creates the target directory and any necessary parent directories if they don't exist.","      * Existing files are overwritten.","      *","+     * On Linux and macOS, nothing is written through a symlink in the target directory that is absolute or leads out of it.","+     * The promise rejects when a file entry needs such a symlink, unless `options.glob` is set, which skips the entry.","+     * The `code` of the error is `\"EXDEV\"`, and its `path` is the path of the entry relative to the target directory.","+     *","      * @param path - The directory path to extract to","      * @param options - Optional extraction options","      * @param options.glob - Glob pattern(s) to filter entries (positive patterns include, negative patterns starting with `!` exclude)","@@ -10843,6 +10847,8 @@ declare module \"bun\" {","      *","      * Only regular files are included; directories are not returned.","      * File contents are loaded into memory, so for large archives consider using `extract()` instead.","+     * The promise rejects once the holes read from the archive's sparse files add up to more than 1,024 times","+     * the size of the archive. Up to 64 MiB are always read, and never more than 1 GiB.","      *","      * @param glob - Optional glob pattern(s) to filter files. Supports the same syntax as {@link Bun.Glob},","      *   including negation patterns (prefixed with `!`). Patterns are matched against paths normalized"],"additions":6,"deletions":0,"truncated":false},{"path":"packages/bun-types/serve.d.ts","lines":["@@ -680,11 +680,12 @@ declare module \"bun\" {","      * Non-canonical paths (containing `.`, `..`, empty segments, `%2F`, or a","      * `%XX` sequence encoding a character that may appear literally in a path","      * segment) are rejected with `404` so the served path is always the path","-     * the router matched. On Linux the open uses `openat2(RESOLVE_IN_ROOT)`,","-     * so symlinks that would escape `dir` are clamped by the kernel. Routing","-     * is case-sensitive but filesystems on macOS and Windows are not by","-     * default: do not place access-controlled content inside `dir` and rely","-     * on an overlapping route to gate it.","+     * the router matched. A symlink never leads out of `dir`: symlinks resolve","+     * as if `dir` were the root directory, so a `..` stops at `dir` and an","+     * absolute target starts from it. Windows follows symlinks wherever they","+     * lead. Routing is case-sensitive but filesystems on macOS and Windows are","+     * not by default: do not place access-controlled content inside `dir` and","+     * rely on an overlapping route to gate it.","      *","      * Responses carry `Content-Type` (from the file extension),","      * `Last-Modified`, a weak `ETag`, and support single-range `Range`"],"additions":6,"deletions":5,"truncated":false},{"path":"src/archive/error.rs","lines":["@@ -10,6 +10,9 @@ pub enum Error {","     MakeLibUvOwned(#[from] bun_sys::MakeLibUvOwnedError),","     #[error(transparent)]","     Paths(#[from] bun_paths::Error),","+    /// `EX